@page "/"

<PageTitle>IntentBlazor — AI application framework</PageTitle>

<p class="eyebrow">OPEN SOURCE · MIT LICENSED</p>
<h1>User intent.<br />A living interface.</h1>
<p class="lead">An AI-centric Blazor framework built around two interaction components.
    Connect your model, expose your C# capabilities, and let the workspace take shape around the task.</p>
<p class="status"><strong>Phase 2 adapters implemented.</strong> Try authored documents and local interactions in the playground,
    or configure an optional model connection. Live model verification remains deferred.</p>
<div class="actions">
    <a class="button" href="/playground">Try the playground</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="/roadmap">Explore the roadmap</a>
</div>

<section class="card-grid" aria-label="Framework direction">
    <article class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow">INPUT</p>
        <h2>Express the task</h2>
        <p>AiInput sends text and current workspace context to a registered input processor. Rich content support is planned.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow">OUTPUT</p>
        <h2>Work with the result</h2>
        <p>AiOutput composes validated interactive views and preserves direct edits. Application actions are planned.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow">DEVELOPER EXPERIENCE</p>
        <h2>Bring your application</h2>
        <p>Attribute and explicit tool registration will expose your services. You own the business rules and surrounding app.</p>
    </article>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Three applications to prove the foundation</h2>
    <p>Gaming News and Tips explores content and sources. Staff Business exercises permissions and shared records.
        Asset Workbench explores previews, edits, and generated artifacts.</p>
    <p>The playground already uses the same document, state, binding, and interaction mechanisms for all three fixture workspaces.
        Live services, authorization, persistence, and standalone apps follow in later phases.</p>
</section>
